text:
Introduction
Every meta-labeling system I've built runs on the same quiet assumption: the label I hand the training set is correct. A candidate signal fired, price moved a certain way over the next N bars, so I call it a win or a loss and feed that pair to a classifier. Nobody stops to check whether the label itself deserves to be there.
It usually doesn't, not entirely. A "win" that only cleared the profit threshold because of a single favorable tick, a "loss" that would have been a win if the exit rule looked five bars further - these aren't edge cases, they're baked into how any mechanical labeling heuristic works. The classifier doesn't know the difference between a clean signal and a coin-flip that happened to land right. It just learns whatever pattern the noisy labels contain, confidently.
The uncomfortable part is that this failure mode is invisible to the usual diagnostics. A meta-filter trained on 50% noisy labels won't throw an error or show unusually high training loss. It may still report plausible accuracy on a held-out set, because that set was labeled with the same noisy heuristic. To see the problem, you have to ask a different question than "does this model fit the data?" Ask instead: "which specific rows are likely mislabeled?" That requires a different kind of audit than a standard train/test split.
That's the gap confident learning fills. It doesn't touch the strategy logic, the entry signal, or the exit rule - it sits between "I have a labeled dataset" and "I train a classifier on it," and its only job is to flag rows where the label probably doesn't match what actually happened. Whether removing those rows helps is a separate, empirical question, and that's the part of this article I was the least prepared for.
This article is about auditing that noise before training, not about a new entry signal. The base strategy here (an RSI/trend crossover) is deliberately simple - it exists to generate a labeled dataset with real, uncurated noise in it. The actual subject is CConfidentLabelAuditor: a native MQL5 implementation of confident learning (Northcutt, Athalye, and Mueller, 2021) that estimates which training labels are probably wrong and removes them before the meta-filter is trained on them.
I built an Expert Advisor with a single input flag,
InpUseCleanedLabels
. It switches meta-filter training between raw labels and the audited (cleaned) subset, while keeping all other inputs identical. That gives a real A/B comparison in the Strategy Tester instead of a claim. I ran it four times: once on raw labels, and three times on cleaned labels at different pruning aggressiveness levels. The results didn't confirm my hunch on the first try, and the honest version of that story is what this article is about.
Where Meta-Label Noise Actually Comes From
The candidate signal itself is a plain RSI/trend crossover - I kept it deliberately unremarkable, since the point of this article is the audit, not the entry rule. A long candidate fires when RSI crosses up through 50 while price sits above its trend SMA; a short candidate mirrors that on the way down:
DetectCandidateSignal
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| DetectCandidateSignal                                            |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| +1 = long candidate (RSI crosses above 50 while price trends up),|
//| -1 = short candidate (mirror image), 0 = no candidate at shift.  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
int
DetectCandidateSignal
(
const
int
shift,
const
double
&close[],
const
double
&sma[],
const
double
&rsi[])
  {
if
(rsi[shift] >= 50.0 && rsi[shift + 1] < 50.0 && close[shift] > sma[shift])
return
(1);
if
(rsi[shift] <= 50.0 && rsi[shift + 1] > 50.0 && close[shift] < sma[shift])
return
(-1);
return
(0);
  }
Every candidate gets a four-number feature vector. Three of the four - RSI trigger strength, distance from trend, and recent momentum - are direction-sign-flipped so long and short candidates share one model instead of needing two. The fourth, the ATR ratio, is a non-directional volatility-regime read and is deliberately left unflipped:
ComputeFeatures
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ComputeFeatures                                                  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| All four features are computed strictly from data available AT   |
//| the signal bar - nothing here ever looks past shift. Direction   |
//| (dir) sign-flips the direction-sensitive features so one filter  |
//| can be trained on both long and short candidates together.       |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
ComputeFeatures
(
const
int
shift,
const
int
dir,
const
double
&close[],
const
double
&sma[],
const
double
&atr[],
const
double
&atr_long[],
const
double
&rsi[],
double
&f[])
  {
double
atr_now = MathMax(atr[shift],CL_EPS);

   f[0] = dir * (rsi[shift] - 50.0) / 50.0;
// RSI trigger strength
f[1] = dir * (close[shift] - sma[shift]) / atr_now;
// distance from trend, in ATRs
f[2] = atr[shift] / MathMax(atr_long[shift],CL_EPS);
// volatility-regime ratio
f[3] = dir * (close[shift] - close[shift + InpMomentumBars]) / atr_now;
// recent momentum
}
And here's the label heuristic itself - the actual source of the noise this whole article is about.
ComputeLabel
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ComputeLabel                                                     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ComputeLabel - the (noisy) meta-label: did price move far enough |
//| in the signal's favor, within InpLabelHorizonBars bars, to clear |
//| InpMinNetMovePoints? This ignores the intrabar path and any      |
//| stop-out that would have happened first - exactly the kind of    |
//| shortcut that seeds label noise, and the reason this EA needs    |
//| the auditor in the first place.                                  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
int
ComputeLabel
(
const
int
shift,
const
int
dir,
const
double
&close[])
  {
int
forward_shift = shift - InpLabelHorizonBars;
double
raw_move_points = dir * (close[forward_shift] - close[shift]) / _Point;
return
(raw_move_points > InpMinNetMovePoints ? 1 : 0);
  }
That single comparison is the entire label. It looks at the close price twenty bars later, checks whether it moved far enough in the signal's favor, and ignores everything in between. A trade that would have been stopped out on bar 3 still reads as a win if price is higher again by bar 20. A trade that was comfortably profitable for fifteen bars still reads as a loss if it gives it all back by bar 20. None of that is a coding mistake - it's what any fixed-horizon forward-return label does, and it's precisely the kind of systematic, structural noise confident learning is built to catch.
The technique doesn't need to know
why
a label might be wrong. It only needs a classifier that can be cross-validated out-of-fold, and it uses that classifier's own disagreement with the given label - measured honestly, on data the classifier never trained on - as the noise signal.
The Confident Learning Math
The pipeline has five steps, and each one maps to a method on
CConfidentLabelAuditor
that I'll show in full later. Here's the reasoning behind each step first, since the code is easier to read once the "why" is in place.
1. Out-of-fold probabilities
. I split the candidate set into k contiguous time blocks (five by default). For each block, I train a fresh logistic regression on the other k-1 blocks and predict on the held-out block. Every sample ends up with a predicted probability from a model that never saw its label during training - that's what makes the next steps trustworthy instead of circular.
Self-confidence for a sample with given label y and out-of-fold probability p₁ (probability of class 1):
self_confidence = p₁ if y = 1, else (1 - p₁)
Per-class threshold (averaged self-confidence a class needs to "confidently" claim a sample):
t_c = mean of self_confidence over every sample whose given (noisy) label is c - grouped by the label being audited, not by what the model currently guesses
2. Per-class thresholds
. Rather than one global cutoff, each class gets its own threshold, computed as the class's own average predicted probability. This is the part of confident learning that lets one class's label noise run hotter than the other's, instead of forcing a single arbitrary cutoff onto both.
3. The confident joint
. For every sample, I check which class's threshold it clears. If both clear, the larger margin above threshold wins the tie. If neither clears, the model has no confident opinion and the sample doesn't vote. Every sample that does vote increments a 2x2 count: confident_joint[given_label][confidently_predicted_label]. The off-diagonal cells are the interesting ones - they're candidate mislabels.
4. Calibration
. The confident joint's rows won't sum to the real number of examples in each class, because the thresholds don't clear on every sample. I rescale each row so it matches the true label counts - this is what corrects for one class's threshold being systematically stricter than the other's.
5. Rank and prune
. For each off-diagonal cell, I take the calibrated count as the estimated number of true mislabels in that cell, and prune exactly that many samples - the ones with the
worst
self-confidence first. This mirrors cleanlab's
prune_counts
approach: prune per-cell by rank, not one flat percentile cut across the whole dataset.
Worth being precise about what "worst self-confidence" means here, since it's the number that actually decides which rows get removed. If a candidate was given label 1 (a "win") but the out-of-fold model only assigns it a 0.05 probability of being a win, its self-confidence is 0.05 - about as strong a disagreement as the model can register. A candidate given label 1 that the model scores at 0.48 is a much weaker disagreement, close to a coin flip, and survives longer in the ranking. The auditor always removes the most confidently-contradicted rows first within each cell, which is what keeps the borderline, ambiguous cases in the training set even under aggressive pruning - it's only the clear-cut mislabels that get cut first.
One more distinction matters. Self-confidence measures how strongly the model supports the given label, on a 0–1 scale - the pruning ranking uses this number, and it's also what the FlagView indicator plots later in this article. Normalized margin measures how much the model prefers the given label over the alternative, on a −1–1 scale. The auditor computes and logs normalized margin to the CSV for anyone who wants to inspect it, but the pruning uses self-confidence only.
System Architecture
Three files, one shared library:
ConfidentLearningLib.mqh
- CLogRegClassifier (the base learner) and CConfidentLabelAuditor (the five-step pipeline above), plus the SConfidentLearningReport struct the audit returns.
ConfidentLearning_Audit_EA.mq5
- builds a labeled candidate set from an RSI/trend crossover signal every InpRefitEveryBars bars, runs the audit, trains the live meta-filter on either the raw or cleaned set depending on InpUseCleanedLabels, and trades whatever that filter approves above InpProbThreshold. Every refit writes two audit CSV logs.
ConfidentLearning_FlagView.mq5
- a separate-window indicator that reads the EA's audit log and plots each audited signal's self-confidence, marking the ones the auditor pruned.
The feature vector is the four numbers shown in the next section: RSI trigger strength, distance from the trend SMA in ATR units, a fast/slow ATR ratio for the volatility regime, and recent momentum - all direction-sign-flipped so long and short candidates train one shared model. The base learner is a small logistic regression trained by batch gradient descent with L2 regularization, used both inside the auditor's cross-fitting and as the final filter the EA actually trades with. Keeping both on the same simple model isolates the effect of label cleaning from any effect of model complexity - if I'd used a bigger model for the final filter than for the fold models, any difference between the raw and cleaned runs could just as easily be attributed to that mismatch instead of to the cleaning itself.
Every refit cycle runs the same six steps in order: scan the last
InpTrainWindowBars
bars for candidate signals, compute features and labels for each one, hand the resulting dataset to
CConfidentLabelAuditor::Audit
, branch on
InpUseCleanedLabels
to decide which rows the final model actually trains on, retrain
g_final_model
, and write both CSV logs. That cycle repeats every
InpRefitEveryBars
bars for the life of the backtest - the model isn't static, it's re-audited and refit on a rolling basis as new bars close.
The Base Learner: CLogRegClassifier
Nothing exotic here - a textbook logistic regression, chosen deliberately small so any performance difference between runs comes from the training data, not from a more expressive model finding structure in the noise.
SConfidentLearningReport struct and CLogRegClassifier declaration
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| SConfidentLearningReport struct                                  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
SConfidentLearningReport
  {
int
n_total;
// candidate signals audited in this refit window
int
n_flagged;
// samples pruned as likely-mislabeled, at the run's
// InpPruneAggressiveness setting
double
est_noise_rate;
// aggressiveness-INDEPENDENT estimate: the calibrated
// joint's off-diagonal mass over n - how much of the
// dataset the auditor believes is mislabeled, before
// any pruning policy is applied to it
double
prune_rate;
// n_flagged / n - how much was ACTUALLY pruned this run,
// which moves with InpPruneAggressiveness and is NOT
// the same number as est_noise_rate except at
// aggressiveness 1.0
double
calibrated_joint[][2];
// calibrated_joint[given_label][confident_label] counts
// (dynamic outer dim - MQL5 requires that for a struct
// member that gets passed by reference into a function)
};
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CLogRegClassifier class                                          |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
class
CLogRegClassifier
{
private
:
double
m_weights[];
// one weight per feature, set by Train()
double
m_bias;
// intercept term
int
m_num_features;
// feature vector length this instance expects
bool
m_ready;
// true once Train() has produced usable weights
public
:
CLogRegClassifier
(
void
);
void
Init
(
const
int
num_features);
double
Sigmoid
(
const
double
z);
bool
Train
(
const
double
&features[][CL_NUM_FEATURES],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
n_samples,
const
int
epochs,
const
double
learning_rate,
const
double
l2);
double
PredictProba
(
const
double
&feature_row[]);
bool
IsReady
(
void
)
const
{
return
(m_ready); }
  };
The training loop is plain batch gradient descent on the logistic loss, with L2 applied only to the weights (never the bias, so the intercept stays free to track the base rate of whatever window it's trained on):
CLogRegClassifier::Train
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CLogRegClassifier::Train                                         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CLogRegClassifier::Train
(
const
double
&features[][CL_NUM_FEATURES],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
n_samples,
const
int
epochs,
const
double
learning_rate,
const
double
l2)
  {
//--- refuse to fit a line through fewer points than the model has
//--- degrees of freedom - a silently-wrong model is worse than none
if
(n_samples < m_num_features + 2)
return
(
false
);

   ArrayInitialize(m_weights,0.0);
   m_bias = 0.0;
double
grad_w[];
   ArrayResize(grad_w,m_num_features);
for
(
int
epoch = 0; epoch < epochs; epoch++)
     {
      ArrayInitialize(grad_w,0.0);
double
grad_b = 0.0;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n_samples; i++)
        {
double
z = m_bias;
for
(
int
j = 0; j < m_num_features; j++)
            z += m_weights[j] * features[i][j];
double
p   =
Sigmoid
(z);
double
err = p - labels[i];
// logistic loss gradient w.r.t. the logit
for
(
int
j = 0; j < m_num_features; j++)
            grad_w[j] += err * features[i][j];
         grad_b += err;
        }
//--- average over the batch, then add the L2 penalty on the weights
//--- only (never on the bias, so the intercept stays free to match
//--- the base rate of the training window)
for
(
int
j = 0; j < m_num_features; j++)
        {
         grad_w[j] = grad_w[j] / n_samples + l2 * m_weights[j];
         m_weights[j] -= learning_rate * grad_w[j];
        }
      grad_b /= n_samples;
      m_bias -= learning_rate * grad_b;
     }

   m_ready =
true
;
return
(
true
);
  }
That first guard -
n_samples < m_num_features + 2
- matters more than it looks. It comes back later in this article, because it's the actual mechanical reason aggressive pruning backfired in my results.
Train
calls
Sigmoid
internally, and
BuildOutOfFold
(next section) calls
Init
and
PredictProba
on each fold's model - all three are short enough to show together instead of scattering them:
CLogRegClassifier::Init, Sigmoid, PredictProba
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CLogRegClassifier::Init, Sigmoid, PredictProba                   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CLogRegClassifier::Init
(
const
int
num_features)
  {
   m_num_features = num_features;
   m_bias         = 0.0;
   m_ready        =
false
;
   ArrayResize(m_weights,num_features);
   ArrayInitialize(m_weights,0.0);
  }
double
CLogRegClassifier::Sigmoid
(
const
double
z)
  {
//--- clamp the exponent so MathExp never overflows on a stray large
//--- logit - the saturated 0.0/1.0 output is the mathematically
//--- correct limit anyway
if
(z < -40.0)
return
(0.0);
if
(z > 40.0)
return
(1.0);
return
(1.0 / (1.0 + MathExp(-z)));
  }
double
CLogRegClassifier::PredictProba
(
const
double
&feature_row[])
  {
double
z = m_bias;
for
(
int
j = 0; j < m_num_features; j++)
      z += m_weights[j] * feature_row[j];
return
(
Sigmoid
(z));
  }
Init
is what makes the k-fold cross-fitting in
BuildOutOfFold
honest - each fold gets a model reset to zero weights, so no fold's out-of-fold prediction ever leaks information from the training rows it was scored against.
Sigmoid
clamps its input before exponentiating for the same reason most people learn to do this the hard way: an unclamped logistic on a stray large logit overflows
MathExp
and hands you a NaN that quietly poisons everything downstream.
The Auditor: CConfidentLabelAuditor
This class is the article. Every method below maps directly to one of the five steps from the math section, pulled verbatim from the same source file that shipped in the archive - nothing here is retyped or paraphrased.
CConfidentLabelAuditor::BuildOutOfFold
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::BuildOutOfFold                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CConfidentLabelAuditor::BuildOutOfFold
(
const
double
&features[][CL_NUM_FEATURES],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
n,
const
int
epochs,
const
double
learning_rate,
const
double
l2,
double
&oof_probs[])
  {
   ArrayResize(oof_probs,n);
   ArrayInitialize(oof_probs,0.5);
// neutral fallback for any row a fold never covers
for
(
int
fold = 0; fold < m_k_folds; fold++)
     {
//--- contiguous-block fold assignment (not a random shuffle): keeping
//--- each fold as one time-ordered block, rather than interleaving
//--- individual bars, limits how much adjacent, autocorrelated bars
//--- leak between a fold's train and held-out sides
int
held_out_count = 0;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
if
((i * m_k_folds) / n == fold)
            held_out_count++;
if
(held_out_count == 0 || held_out_count == n)
continue
;
double
train_features[][CL_NUM_FEATURES];
int
train_labels[];
      ArrayResize(train_features,n - held_out_count);
      ArrayResize(train_labels,n - held_out_count);
int
t = 0;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
if
((i * m_k_folds) / n == fold)
continue
;
for
(
int
j = 0; j < m_num_features; j++)
            train_features[t][j] = features[i][j];
         train_labels[t] = labels[i];
         t++;
        }

      CLogRegClassifier fold_model;
      fold_model.
Init
(m_num_features);
if
(!fold_model.
Train
(train_features,train_labels,t,epochs,learning_rate,l2))
continue
;
double
row[];
      ArrayResize(row,m_num_features);
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
if
((i * m_k_folds) / n != fold)
continue
;
for
(
int
j = 0; j < m_num_features; j++)
            row[j] = features[i][j];
         oof_probs[i] = fold_model.
PredictProba
(row);
        }
     }
  }
CConfidentLabelAuditor::ComputeThresholds and BuildConfidentJoint
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::ComputeThresholds                        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CConfidentLabelAuditor::ComputeThresholds
(
const
double
&oof_probs[],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
n,
double
&t0,
double
&t1)
  {
double
sum0 = 0.0, sum1 = 0.0;
int
n0 = 0, n1 = 0;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
     {
if
(labels[i] == 1)
        {
         sum1 += oof_probs[i];
         n1++;
        }
else
{
         sum0 += (1.0 - oof_probs[i]);
         n0++;
        }
     }
//--- each class thresholds itself off its own average predicted
//--- probability - this is what lets one class's noise level differ
//--- from the other's instead of sharing a single arbitrary cutoff
t1 = (n1 > 0 ? sum1 / n1 : 0.5);
   t0 = (n0 > 0 ? sum0 / n0 : 0.5);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::BuildConfidentJoint                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CConfidentLabelAuditor::BuildConfidentJoint
(
const
double
&oof_probs[],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
n,
const
double
t0,
const
double
t1,
int
&confident_joint[][2],
int
&confident_class[])
  {
   ArrayResize(confident_joint,2);
// dynamic outer dim - size it before first use
confident_joint[0][0] = 0; confident_joint[0][1] = 0;
   confident_joint[1][0] = 0; confident_joint[1][1] = 0;
   ArrayResize(confident_class,n);
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
     {
double
p1 = oof_probs[i];
double
p0 = 1.0 - p1;
bool
passes0 = (p0 >= t0);
bool
passes1 = (p1 >= t1);
if
(!passes0 && !passes1)
        {
//--- neither class clears its own threshold - the model has no
//--- confident opinion on this sample, so it cannot vote either way
confident_class[i] = -1;
continue
;
        }
if
(passes0 && passes1)
        {
//--- both clear - break the tie toward whichever class the model is
//--- confident in by the larger margin above its own threshold
confident_class[i] = ((p1 - t1) > (p0 - t0) ? 1 : 0);
        }
else
{
         confident_class[i] = (passes1 ? 1 : 0);
        }

      confident_joint[labels[i]][confident_class[i]]++;
     }
  }
Calibration rescales each row of that joint against the real per-class counts, so a stricter threshold on one class doesn't make it look artificially cleaner than the other:
CConfidentLabelAuditor::CalibrateJoint
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::CalibrateJoint                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CConfidentLabelAuditor::CalibrateJoint
(
const
int
&confident_joint[][2],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
n,
double
&calibrated_joint[][2])
  {
   ArrayResize(calibrated_joint,2);
// dynamic outer dim - size it before first use
int
row_sum[2];
   row_sum[0] = confident_joint[0][0] + confident_joint[0][1];
   row_sum[1] = confident_joint[1][0] + confident_joint[1][1];
int
label_count[2] = {0, 0};
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
      label_count[labels[i]]++;
//--- rescale each row so it sums to the ACTUAL number of examples
//--- given that label, not to however many the thresholds happened
//--- to rule confidently on - this is what corrects for one class's
//--- threshold being stricter than the other's
for
(
int
i = 0; i < 2; i++)
     {
for
(
int
j = 0; j < 2; j++)
        {
if
(row_sum[i] > 0)
            calibrated_joint[i][j] = (
double
)confident_joint[i][j] *
                                      ((
double
)label_count[i] / (
double
)row_sum[i]);
else
calibrated_joint[i][j] = 0.0;
        }
     }
  }
RankAndFlag
leans on two small private helpers for the two numbers it logs per sample - shown here since they're called, not just described:
CConfidentLabelAuditor::SelfConfidence and NormalizedMargin
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::SelfConfidence                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CConfidentLabelAuditor::SelfConfidence
(
const
double
p1,
const
int
given_label)
  {
//--- probability the model out-of-fold assigns to the label the
//--- sample was actually GIVEN - low means the model confidently
//--- disagrees with the trainer's own label
return
(given_label == 1 ? p1 : 1.0 - p1);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::NormalizedMargin                         |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CConfidentLabelAuditor::NormalizedMargin
(
const
double
p1,
const
int
given_label)
  {
double
p_given = (given_label == 1 ? p1 : 1.0 - p1);
double
p_other = (given_label == 1 ? 1.0 - p1 : p1);
return
(p_given - p_other);
  }
And the actual pruning - worst self-confidence first, per off-diagonal cell, up to that cell's calibrated count:
CConfidentLabelAuditor::RankAndFlag
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CConfidentLabelAuditor::RankAndFlag                              |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CConfidentLabelAuditor::RankAndFlag
(
const
double
&oof_probs[],
const
int
&labels[],
const
int
&confident_class[],
const
int
n,
const
double
&calibrated_joint[][2],
bool
&is_flagged[],
double
&self_conf[],
double
&norm_margin[])
  {
   ArrayResize(is_flagged,n);
   ArrayResize(self_conf,n);
